Byron A Jeff wrote:
> Monsters get tougher the deeper you go and the more experienced you
> get. So you need to take care in levelling up.

Indeed. It is really worthwhile learning how to let your
pet do almost all the killing. With a spellcaster, I try
to kill newts myself, and let the pet have all the others.
It isn't unusual for my PC to find the minestown priest
while still playing with single digit XP and to _finish_
Sokoban at XL3 or lower (shove the pets into the zoo
and remain outside the door putting monsters to sleep
and waiting for the dust to settle.

If I lose my pet, though, a completely different, and much
less successful, strategy is needed.

It is really, really important to keep DLVL +XL low, which
in practice means keeping XL low, until you can tame or
polymorph buffer pets, because there comes a point
where your (DLVL +XL)/2 factor is creating monsters your
pet will happily leave for you to kill, and then your XP and
XL will skyrocket out of control until some monster outside
your capabilities kills you because your pets refuse to
help.  [IIUC,  pets won't attack anything more than two
levels higher than themselves, even if they outnumber
the monster five to one, and even if it is down to its last
hit point.]

Tips on keeping XP low:

Zapping sleep or slow monster doesn't gain you any XP,
so save those wands and use them when running away
fails, to give your pet time to kill the monster.

Using low damage weapons in concert with your pet
against the same monster, increases the chances that
it will be your pet that kills the monster, at the same
time it increases the chances that your pet will
_survive_ to kill the monster. Any blow you strike that
injures but doesn't kill the monster gains you no XP.

Watch your own HP, you can afford to give some away
while your pet is running to your rescue, just judge
carefully how long to keep "turning the other cheek".
Being a punching bag doesn't gain you any XP.

Anything to speed yourself up -- staying unburdened,
zapping speed monster at yourself and your pet,
wearing speed boots -- lets you run away more
successfully. Running away doesn't gain you any XP,
but run at a real clock snail's pace so you don't
run over something easily killed.

Displace your pet into harms way, but if able, watch
your pet's HPs too, and spend healing power on your
pet in preference to yourself. Assisting your pet to
survive doesn't gain you any XP.

Monsters are _really_ stupid. Dance around a trap,
and the monster will often try to walk through it to you.

This is especially fun for holes, trapdoors, pits, and
teleport traps, which don't wear out or run out of
ammunition, and immobilize the monster or put
it somewhere else where you can cope with it better
later. Luring a monster into a trap doesn't gain you
any XP (even if the monster dies), and may injure
the monster enough that your pet can kill it.

Dance circles around your pet. This often works even
if two monsters are attacking the pair of you. Monsters
on the far side of your pet don't melee you, so you
don't get XP killing them.

Displace your pet in a corridor to put it between you
and the monsters, ditto.

Do what is needed to keep your pet alive. In Mines Town,
where your pets are probably willing to attack the
watch captain but unlikely to survive the attempt, keep
them on leash or locked in a room until you are ready
to leave, and while easy monsters for them to kill are
absent, or displace them away from the watch captain,
or magic whistle them away from danger, as needed.
Dead pets can't help you avoid gaining XP.

Pay particular attention to pets that suddenly start
eating a corpse, and stop fighting on your behalf. This
is a really good time to run circles around that pet until
it can resume soaking up the XP you are trying to avoid.

I'm sure others can add other tricks to this small sample.

